-- CONTROL for b1-xmod-same-spelled-iface-impl-selection, promoted from that
-- must_fail pin's own `control.mdk`. ONE dimension varied: whether the two
-- unrelated modules' interface/type/method spellings COLLIDE.
--
-- amod.mdk is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the sibling's. zmod2.mdk keeps the sibling zmod's
-- shape and arithmetic byte-for-byte and changes only the three spellings
-- (`Same2`/`Blob2`/`sizeOf2`), so there is no cross-module spelling collision left.
-- Everything else -- the module topology, the import clauses, the bystander, the
-- three printed values -- is held fixed.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER: identical to the sibling's, and hand-derived the same way. Two
-- differently-spelled interfaces are two classes for the uninteresting reason as
-- well as the interesting one, each goal has a singleton matching set, so
-- 11 / 110 / 7.
--
-- ⚠️ THE CONTROL'S VALUE IS THE SAME AS THE SIBLING'S, ON PURPOSE, and that is
-- what it asserts: the answer must not depend on the spelling collision. So this
-- pair is read by which member reds, not by their values differing. Sibling RED +
-- control GREEN localises a regression to the collision; BOTH red says
-- cross-module dispatch broke generally and the collision is a red herring. Before
-- the drain the sibling's `run` printed 11 / 11 / 7 while this file already printed
-- 11 / 110 / 7 on the same binary -- that measured contrast is the whole reason the
-- defect was attributable to the collision rather than to module boundaries.
import amod.{aValue}
import zmod2.{zValue2}
import bystander.{bystanderValue}

main =
  let _ = println aValue
  let _ = println zValue2
  println bystanderValue
